-- CK-16: one explicit `Basics.eq` asks one question, and says "no" once
-- (checker-v2.md §11.4 *As built by R5*, I13; R5's structural review, B1).
-- The comparison's walk flags `x` and `y`; `[ x, y ]` merges them; `x 1`
-- makes the merged variable a function. The two flags carry the same
-- question, so ONE `not_equatable`, at the comparison's argument — not one
-- per flag (R5 as first built printed the same message twice). On checker v1
-- at 2bc9e22 the program checks clean: the walk accepts a variable inside a
-- structure (CK-16's own defect).
h x y =
    let
        t =
            Basics.eq ( x, y ) ( x, y )

        l =
            [ x, y ]
    in
    x 1
